findings.finding:
Round 5 adopts the allowlist R4-1 asked for, and every round-4 probe is now refused. But one allowlisted word carries systemd unit configuration in its value, and three allowlisted words choose the tree that systemd boots.

(a) rootflags= adds units in a systemd initrd. The pattern rootflags=[A-Za-z0-9_.,=-]+ admits systemd's x-systemd.* and libmount's X-mount.* mount options. In systemd v256 src/fstab-generator/fstab-generator.c:
- parse_proc_cmdline_item appends every rootflags= value to arg_root_options;
- in the initrd, add_sysroot_mount passes those options for /sysroot to add_mount;
- add_mount calls write_extra_dependencies. x-systemd.requires=<unit> becomes Requires=<unit> and After=<unit> on sysroot.mount. x-systemd.after= and x-systemd.before= add ordering;
- x-systemd.wanted-by= and x-systemd.required-by= are honoured, because add_mount drops them only for '/' and '/usr', not for '/sysroot';
- x-systemd.makefs hooks up systemd-makefs@.service.
The model ACCEPTS each of the following probes, each with every capture rebound:
- G01: ENROLLED plus 'rootflags=x-systemd.requires=planeon-maintenance.target';
- G02: the same with emergency.target;
- G03 to G05: x-systemd.wanted-by=, x-systemd.after=/before= and x-systemd.makefs;
- GM1: MAINTENANCE plus 'rootflags=x-systemd.requires=multi-user.target'.
So the initrd's PID 1 starts a unit that a command-line word names. That is the initrd scope in which V57 refuses rd.systemd.unit=. On the host, fstab-generator does not use arg_root_options. gpt-auto-generator copies rootflags only into Options=, and src/core/mount.c passes Options= to mount(8), which does not add dependencies. The effect therefore ends at switch-root, unless the named unit ships in the initrd, as emergency.target does in systemd initrds.

(b) Selection of the tree. The same words pick the tree whose installed state the K1 statement leaves to W03:
- 'rootflags=X-mount.subdir=<dir>' (util-linux 2.39 and later; sysroot.mount runs mount(8) with Options=) mounts a subdirectory as /sysroot;
- btrfs 'rootflags=subvol=<name>' mounts a subvolume as /sysroot;
- nothing ties the enrolled entry's root=, rootflags=, rootfstype= or initrd= words to the maintenance entry's.
All of these probes are ACCEPTED: G06 X-mount.subdir=, G07 subvol=, G09 another root partition, G11 another initrd, G12 a second initrd, G13 no root= (gpt-auto discovery in the initrd). So the enrolled entry can boot a different root, whose default.target link may be the maintenance target.

Neither path weakens lockdown or SELinux:
- SELinux context mount options need ':', which the rootflags= pattern refuses (G19, G20);
- selinux=, enforcing= and lockdown= admit only their enabling values;
- the pinned booleans remain the backstop.
The README's closing sentence of the K1 row, the model comment and decision 2's premise (a grammar of named kernel parameters closes unit selection) are therefore not accurate.

